.TH SNAFFLER 1 "November 2026" "snaffler-ng 1.5.13" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
snaffler \- discover credentials and sensitive data on SMB shares, FTP servers, and local filesystems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snaffler
.RB [ \-u
.IR USERNAME ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR PASSWORD ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR DOMAIN ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.PP
.B snaffler results
.RB [ \-s
.IR DB ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR FORMAT ]
.RI [ SUBCOMMAND ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snaffler
(snaffler-ng) is a post-exploitation tool that discovers readable SMB shares,
FTP servers, and local directories, walks their trees, and identifies
credentials and sensitive data using rule-based matching. It is an
Impacket-based Python port of the original C# Snaffler.
.PP
The tool operates in four pipeline stages:
.IP 1. 4
.B Domain Discovery
\- queries Active Directory via LDAP for computer objects and DFS namespaces
.IP 2. 4
.B Share Discovery
\- enumerates SMB shares on target hosts and checks readability (and write access)
.IP 3. 4
.B File Scanning
\- walks directory trees and classifies files using rule-based matching
.IP 4. 4
.B FTP Scanning
\- connects to FTP/FTPS servers and walks their directory trees
.PP
Findings are assigned a triage severity:
.B Black
(critical),
.B Red
(high),
.B Yellow
(medium), or
.B Green
(low).
.PP
Scan state is persisted to a SQLite database. If an existing state DB is
found, the scan automatically resumes from where it left off. Use
.B \-\-fresh
to start clean.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show help message and exit.
.SS Authentication
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-username " " \fITEXT\fR
Username for authentication.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-password " " \fITEXT\fR
Password for authentication (NTLM).
.TP
.BI \-\-hash " TEXT"
NT hash for Pass-the-Hash authentication.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-kerberos
Use Kerberos authentication. Requires \fB\-d\fR and hostnames/FQDNs
as targets.
.TP
.B \-\-use\-kcache
Use Kerberos credentials from ccache (reads \fBKRB5CCNAME\fR environment
variable).
.SS Targeting
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-domain " " \fITEXT\fR
Target Active Directory domain or Kerberos realm (e.g. CORP.LOCAL).
When provided without \fB\-\-computer\fR or \fB\-\-unc\fR, triggers full domain
discovery via LDAP.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-computer " " \fITEXT\fR
Target computer(s) by hostname, IP, CIDR (10.0.0.0/24), or range
(10.0.0.1\-50). Can be specified multiple times.
Kerberos requires names, not IPs.
.TP
.BI \-\-computer\-file " PATH"
File containing computer names, one per line.
.TP
.BI \-\-unc " TEXT"
Direct UNC path(s) to scan. Disables computer/share discovery. Can be
specified multiple times.
.TP
.BI \-\-local\-fs " PATH"
Local directory path(s) to scan. Scans the local filesystem instead of SMB.
Can be specified multiple times. Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-unc\fR,
\fB\-\-computer\fR, \fB\-\-domain\fR, and \fB\-\-stdin\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-ftp " URL"
FTP target URL(s) to scan (e.g. ftp://10.0.0.5/data or bare 10.0.0.5).
Can be specified multiple times.
.TP
.BI \-\-ftp\-file " PATH"
File containing FTP targets (one per line, same format as \fB\-\-ftp\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-ftp\-tls
Use FTPS (FTP over TLS) for \fB\-\-ftp\fR targets.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-shares\-only
Only enumerate shares, skip filesystem walking.
.TP
.B \-\-stdin
Read NetExec (nxc) SMB \fB\-\-shares\fR output from stdin and use as UNC targets.
.TP
.B \-\-grab
Bulk download mode: read file paths from stdin, download to \fB\-m\fR directory
(no scanning).
.TP
.B \-\-include\-disabled
Include disabled and stale (4+ months inactive) computer accounts. By
default these are skipped.
.TP
.BI \-\-share " GLOB"
Only scan shares matching glob pattern (case-insensitive, repeatable).
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude\-share " GLOB"
Skip shares matching glob pattern (case-insensitive, repeatable).
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude\-unc " GLOB"
Skip paths matching glob pattern (case-insensitive, repeatable). Also
available as \fB\-\-exclude\-path\fR. Works with both UNC and local paths.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclusions " PATH"
File of hostnames/IPs to skip (one per line).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-hosts " INTEGER"
Stop after scanning N hosts (applied after exclusions).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-check\-writable
Skip the per-share write-access probe (RW detection is on by default).
.TP
.B \-\-check\-writable
Force-enable the per-share write-access probe (overrides a config
\fBcheck_writable = false\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-rescan\-unreadable
Re-test previously unreadable shares from state DB with current creds.
.TP
.B \-\-domain\-users
Discover interesting AD users and match their names in file contents.
Requires \fB\-d\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-user\-match " TEXT"
Keyword filter for interesting usernames (repeatable; default:
sql,svc,service,...).
.TP
.BI \-\-user\-min\-len " INTEGER"
Minimum username length for dynamic user rules. Default: 6.
.SS Network
.TP
.BI \-\-dc\-host " TEXT"
Domain controller hostname, FQDN, or IP address.
Hostnames are required for Kerberos LDAP.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " INTEGER"
SMB timeout in seconds. Default: 5.
.TP
.BI \-\-socks " URL"
Route all connections through a SOCKS proxy for pivoting. Accepts
\fBsocks5://host:port\fR, \fBsocks4://host:port\fR, or bare \fBhost:port\fR
(defaults to SOCKS5). Authenticated proxies use \fBsocks5://user:pass@host:port\fR.
Requires the \fBpysocks\fR package (\fBpip install snaffler-ng[socks]\fR).
DNS resolution is performed on the proxy side so internal hostnames resolve correctly.
.TP
.BI \-\-nameserver " IP"
Custom DNS server for hostname resolution (e.g. DC IP). Also available
as \fB\-\-ns\fR.
.SS Output
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIPATH\fR
Write results to file.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " [debug|info|data]"
Log level. Default: info. The \fBdata\fR level shows findings only.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-log\-type " " \fI[plain|json|tsv]\fR
Output format. Auto-detected from \fB\-o\fR file extension if omitted;
falls back to plain.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-no\-banner
Disable startup banner.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colored output.
.SS Scanning
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-min\-interest " " \fIINTEGER\fR
Minimum interest level to report. 0=Green (all), 1=Yellow, 2=Red, 3=Black
(critical only). Default: 0.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-max\-read\-bytes " " \fIINTEGER\fR
Maximum bytes to read from a file for content scanning. Default: 2097152 (2 MB).
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-max\-file\-bytes " " \fIINTEGER\fR
Maximum file size allowed for scanning and downloading. Default: 10485760 (10 MB).
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-snaffle\-path " " \fIPATH\fR
Directory to copy interesting files into.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-context " " \fIINTEGER\fR
Bytes of context around matched strings. Default: 200.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-depth " INTEGER"
Maximum directory recursion depth (0 = share root only). Unlimited by default.
.TP
.B \-\-fast
Skip known time-waster directories and interleave share walking.
.TP
.BI \-\-match " REGEX"
Only output findings matching this regex (applied to path, rule, match,
and context).
.SS Advanced
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-max\-threads " " \fIINTEGER\fR
Maximum total worker threads, split equally across share/tree/file stages.
Default: 60.
.TP
.BI \-\-dns\-threads " INTEGER"
Concurrent threads for DNS and port 445 reachability probes. Default: 100.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-threads\-per\-share " INTEGER"
Max concurrent tree-walk threads per share (0 = unlimited; \fB\-\-fast\fR
auto-sets this). Default: 0.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to TOML configuration file.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-rule\-dir " " \fIPATH\fR
Directory containing custom TOML rule files.
.TP
.B \-\-stealth
OPSEC mode: pad LDAP queries with random attributes to break IDS signatures.
.TP
.BI \-\-state " PATH"
Path to state database. Default: ./snaffler.db. If the DB exists, the scan
automatically resumes from it.
.TP
.B \-\-fresh
Ignore existing state DB and start a clean scan.
.SS Web Dashboard
.TP
.B \-\-web
Enable live web dashboard during scan. Requires \fBpip install snaffler-ng[web]\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-web\-port " INTEGER"
Port for web dashboard. Default: 8080.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.SS snaffler results
Display stats and findings from a scan state database.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-state " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to scan state database. Default: snaffler.db.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fI[plain|json|html]\fR
Output format. Default: plain.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-min\-interest " " \fIINTEGER\fR
Minimum severity to show (0=all, 1=Yellow+, 2=Red+, 3=Black only).
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rule " " \fINAME\fR
Filter findings by rule name (repeatable).
.TP
.BR \-hu ", " \-\-hide\-unreadable
Hide unreadable shares section.
.TP
.B \-\-files
Print one file path per line (pipe into \fB\-\-grab\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colored output.
.SS snaffler results rules
List all matching rules and their finding counts.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fI[plain|json]\fR
Output format.
.SS snaffler results export
Export scan results as a gzip-compressed DB or JSON file.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler results export findings.json
snaffler results export backup.db
.fi
.RE
.SS snaffler results import
Import and merge findings from another scan DB or JSON export.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler results import other_scan.db
snaffler results import findings.json
.fi
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Full domain discovery (LDAP computer enumeration + DFS + share scan):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u jsmith -p 'P@ssw0rd' -d CORP.LOCAL
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan specific UNC paths:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u admin -p pass --unc //10.0.0.5/HR --unc //10.0.0.5/Finance
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan multiple computers with share discovery:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u admin -p pass -c dc01 -c fs01
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan a CIDR range:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u admin -p pass -c 10.0.0.0/24
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan local filesystem directories:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler --local-fs /mnt/share --local-fs /opt/backups
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan FTP servers:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u ftpuser -p pass --ftp ftp://10.0.0.5/data --ftp 10.0.0.6
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan FTP over TLS:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u ftpuser -p pass --ftp 10.0.0.5 --ftp-tls
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pass-the-Hash authentication:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u admin --hash aad3b435b51404eeaad3b435b51404ee -d CORP.LOCAL
.fi
.RE
.PP
Kerberos with ccache:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
export KRB5CCNAME=/tmp/krb5cc_jsmith
snaffler -k --use-kcache -d CORP.LOCAL --dc-host CORP-DC01
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pipe from NetExec:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
nxc smb 10.0.0.0/24 -u admin -p pass --shares | snaffler -u admin -p pass --stdin
.fi
.RE
.PP
JSON output, Red+ findings only, write to file:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u admin -p pass -d CORP.LOCAL -t json -b 2 -o findings.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fast scan with depth limit and web dashboard:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u admin -p pass -d CORP.LOCAL --fast --max-depth 3 --web
.fi
.RE
.PP
Filter shares and exclude paths:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u admin -p pass -c fs01 --share 'HR*' --exclude-unc '*\\Windows\\*'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Stealth mode with custom DNS:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u admin -p pass -d CORP.LOCAL --stealth --nameserver 10.0.0.1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pivot through a SOCKS proxy (e.g. Chisel, Ligolo, SSH tunnel):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u admin -p pass -d CORP.LOCAL --socks socks5://127.0.0.1:1080
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fresh scan (ignore previous state):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler -u admin -p pass --computer-file targets.txt --fresh
.fi
.RE
.PP
View results from scan database:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler results
snaffler results -b 2 -f json
snaffler results --files | snaffler -u admin -p pass --grab -m ./loot
snaffler results rules
snaffler results export backup.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Bulk download flagged files:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
snaffler results --files -b 2 | snaffler -u admin -p pass --grab -m ./loot
.fi
.RE
.SH TRIAGE LEVELS
.TP
.B Black (3)
Critical: credentials, private keys, hash dumps.
.TP
.B Red (2)
High: config files with secrets, connection strings.
.TP
.B Yellow (1)
Medium: potentially interesting files.
.TP
.B Green (0)
Low: mildly interesting.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I snaffler.db
Default SQLite state database (created in current directory). Enables
automatic scan resume.
.TP
.I rules/*.toml
Custom TOML rule files loaded via \fB\-\-rule\-dir\fR.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B KRB5CCNAME
Path to Kerberos credential cache, used with \fB\-\-use\-kcache\fR.
